In lattice quantum chromodynamics (lattice QCD), the nucleon isovector axial charge $g_A$ is defined as the zero-momentum-transfer value of the nucleon's isovector axial-vector form factor, equivalently the matrix element $\langle p,s | \bar u \gamma^\mu \gamma_5 d | n,s \rangle$ between proton and neutron states at rest, normalized by the conventional spin factor. Numerical evaluation of $g_A$ on a finite Euclidean lattice with spacing $a$, spatial extent $L$, and pion mass $m_\pi$ is affected by four systematic effects:

(i) excited-state contamination (ESC): contributions from multi-hadron states (notably $\pi N$ and $\pi\pi N$ states) to nucleon two- and three-point correlation functions at finite Euclidean separation;
(ii) pion-mass extrapolation: dependence of $g_A$ on the simulated quark mass and the need to extrapolate or interpolate to the physical point $m_\pi \approx 135\text{ MeV}$;
(iii) finite-volume effects: deviations of order $e^{-m_\pi L}$ or in powers of $1/L$ between the finite-box and infinite-volume values;
(iv) continuum extrapolation: removal of discretization artifacts as $a \to 0$.

Devise lattice-QCD methodologies (choices of interpolating operators, source–sink separations, multi-state and variational analyses, ensemble layouts in $(a, m_\pi, L)$, and chiral/continuum/finite-volume fit forms) together with a quantitative error budget that simultaneously controls (i)–(iv) so that:

(a) using computational resources available with current (pre-exascale) high-performance computing architectures, the total uncertainty on $g_A$ can be reduced to approximately $0.5\%$ (i.e.\ $\delta g_A / g_A \lesssim 5\times 10^{-3}$);

(b) using exascale-era computational resources, the total uncertainty can be further reduced to approximately $0.2\%$ (i.e.\ $\delta g_A / g_A \lesssim 2\times 10^{-3}$).

Provide a concrete strategy and demonstrate, by scaling estimates of statistical and systematic errors, that the stated precisions are achievable.

